    perf(executor): Phase B Step 3 follow-up #3 — wire commit/rollback to lockfree paths

    After follow-up #4 (UnsafeCell) made `*_transaction_lockfree(&self)`
    truly sound, the executor's `commit_transaction` and
    `rollback_transaction` were still using the legacy
    `storage.write()` + `storage.commit_transaction()` path, defeating the
    point of the lockfree variants introduced in Step 3.

    This commit wires those paths to the lockfree variants:

    * `commit_transaction`:
      - Calls `commit_transaction_lockfree()` first (no global write
        lock — only the inner `parking_lot::Mutex<wal>`).
      - Only escalates to `storage.write()` for `release_all_gap_locks`
        (which still needs `&mut self`). The WAL append + tx_id clear
        already happened in the lockfree path.
      - Falls back to legacy `commit_transaction` for engines that haven't
        implemented the lockfree variant (default impl returns Err).
    * `rollback_transaction`:
      - Calls `rollback_transaction_lockfree()` first under
        `self.storage.read()` — this discards buffered writes and clears
        tx state without the global write lock.
      - The per-record undo replay (`transaction_manager.rollback_with_undo`)
        still uses the closure pattern that takes `storage.write()` per
        record (same as before — it's already lock-friendly).

    SOAK (oltp_read_write, wal_sync=off, 8 threads, 10k rows, 120s):
    - OLD (UnsafeCell only): 2016.6 TPS
    - NEW (UnsafeCell + lockfree commit/rollback wiring): 2008.32 TPS
    - Δ: -0.4% (noise — both paths were already optimal for BEGIN/COMMIT
      hot path; the gap-lock path is the residual).

    The architectural win is correctness: the lockfree paths are now the
    default in the executor. Future Step 4 (MVCC) and Step 5 (BufferPool)
    work will compose on top of this without further executor changes.

