    Eliminates the `Arc<RwLock<BoxStorageEngine>>` read-lock contention on
    SELECTs (the dominant bottleneck identified by Phase B profiling:
    `parking_lot::RawRwLock::lock_shared_slow` accounts for ~45-68% of
    `execute_select` samples).

    After MVCC: SELECTs acquire only a snapshot timestamp atomically
    (one `AtomicU64::load`), then iterate through version chains without
    holding any lock. Writers append new versions under the existing
    `WalStorage` write lock.

    ## Architecture

    - `crates/storage/src/mvcc.rs` (~430 lines + 11 unit tests):
      - `VersionedRow { row, visible_from_ts, created_by_tx, deleted }`
      - `VersionedTable`: BTreeMap<PK, Vec<VersionedRow>> with global
        `AtomicU64` snapshot counter, per-chain visibility filtering,
        GC for old versions, tombstone support.
      - Visibility rule: `v.visible_from_ts <= snapshot_ts` AND not
        tombstoned. Chain scans from newest to oldest.
      - GC policy: drop versions older than `current - GC_LAG` (default
        1024) keeping at minimum the newest tombstone.

    - `crates/storage/src/mvcc_storage.rs` (~470 lines + 5 unit tests):
      - `MvccStorage<S: StorageEngine>` wraps an inner engine
        (typically `FileStorage`).
      - `scan` / `scan_with_filter` acquire a snapshot timestamp
        atomically (one `AtomicU64::load`) and read MVCC chains.
      - `insert` / `update` / `delete` / `force_insert` write to inner
        AND append MVCC versions under `next_snapshot_ts`.
      - `rebuild_from_inner` rebuilds MVCC state from inner engine
        (used at startup and after WAL recovery).

